# Benchmark scene data (see "Real scenes" in CLAUDE.md) — large binaries in LFS.
# OBJs are committed zstd-compressed (.obj.zst, ~6x — OBJ is ASCII text; the
# loader decodes transparently); the plain-.obj pattern stays as a guard so an
# accidental re-add of an uncompressed mesh can't bloat plain git history.
# Scoped to scenes/ so the repo-root check*.png verification images stay plain git.
scenes/**/*.zst filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.obj filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# Textures are committed as LOSSLESS WebP (~30% under PNG; the loader falls
# back foo.png -> foo.webp when the manifest-referenced PNG is absent). The
# png pattern above stays as the accidental-re-add guard.
scenes/**/*.webp filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# glTF scene data (Khronos/Intel Sponza, Bistro, ... — see "Real scenes"):
# GLB containers, external buffer blobs, and JPEG textures (glTF scenes often
# ship JPEGs where the OBJ archive uses PNG).
scenes/**/*.glb filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.bin filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.jpg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.jpeg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.gltf filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# Environment maps and any straggler binary texture formats a scene drop might
# carry — LFS guards so nothing binary reaches plain git history by accident.
scenes/**/*.hdr filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.exr filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.tga filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.dds filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# Ambience loops (scenes/audio/ — CC0, one per curated world island; see
# src/audio.rs and scenes/audio/credits_license.txt). OGG is the committed
# format; the wav/mp3/flac patterns are accidental-re-add guards for raw
# downloads that should never reach plain git history.
scenes/**/*.ogg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.wav filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.mp3 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
scenes/**/*.flac filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# The --check-wgsl W7 corpus golden (goldens/web_corpus.txt): a tracked TEXT
# golden byte-compared by the gate on the dev box AND the check-vulkan CI job.
# PINNED -text so the blob's LF bytes survive every checkout unmangled — the
# writer emits LF and the compare strips \r as belt-and-braces, but a golden
# that round-trips through autocrlf would still dirty `git status` after a
# --write-golden, which reads exactly like a regression (the check-<tag>.png
# lesson).
goldens/* -text
# Git hooks (tools/hooks/, installed per clone into .git/hooks — see the header
# in tools/hooks/pre-commit). PINNED TO LF: a shell script checked out with
# CRLF has "#!/bin/sh\r" as its shebang, which is a "bad interpreter" failure
# on every non-Windows clone. eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf for these.
tools/hooks/* text eol=lf
# Curated README/release media, rendered by --cinematic. Deliberately PLAIN GIT
# and NOT covered by the scenes/ LFS rules above: github.com renders these on
# the front page, so they must survive a GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE clone, a fork, and
# an exhausted LFS bandwidth quota — and the LFS budget belongs to scenes/.
# Video is NEVER committed HERE, for two independent measured reasons. (1) A
# README cannot embed a player: GitHub's README renderer STRIPS <video> --
# fetching the live page via repos/.../readme with Accept:
# application/vnd.github.html finds zero <video> elements while the <img> beside
# it survives. Beware the standalone /markdown API, which is more permissive and
# returns the element intact, so testing there says the opposite of what ships.
# (2) The bytes must be served playably: raw.githubusercontent.com sends
# application/octet-stream WITH X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, and a release
# asset is an `attachment` behind a signed URL that expires in an hour. So the
# videos live on the orphan `gh-pages` branch, which Pages serves as a real
# video/mp4 with byte ranges, and the README LINKS to them. A separate branch
# rather than /docs on master because Pages publishes its whole source tree and
# docs/ holds third-party academic PDFs. The 4K60 HDR10 master stays a Release
# asset.
docs/media/** binary
# Reference papers the design is measured against (docs/papers/). PLAIN GIT for
# the same reason as docs/media/ above: the README links them, so a reader
# clicking through on github.com must get the file itself — not an LFS
# "stored with Git LFS" interstitial, and not a broken link on a fork, a
# GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE clone, or an exhausted LFS bandwidth quota. `binary`
# only stops git from trying to diff them as text. Keep this directory small
# and additive: a paper or two, never a library. They are third-party
# publications — see docs/papers/README.md for provenance.
docs/papers/** binary
